To solve this problem, you can use a hash table to keep track of the previously seen values in the array. As you iterate through the array, you can check if the current value is already in the hash table and if so, check if the absolute difference between the current index and the index of the previously seen value is less than or equal to k. If so, you have found a match and can return true. If you iterate through the entire array without finding a match, return false. Here is the code for the solution:

```
def containsNearbyDuplicate(nums, k):
    seen_values = {}
    for i in range(len(nums)):
        if nums[i] in seen_values and abs(i - seen_values[nums[i]]) <= k:
            return True
        seen_values[nums[i]] = i
    return False
```

